"""
Design Memo:

A graph is the right data structure for representing a social
network because people can have connections with many different
individuals. Each person represents a node, and each friendship
represents an edge connecting two nodes. An adjacency list works
well because it stores each person's friends directly in a list.
This makes it easy to add friendships and see who is connected.

A regular list would not work as well because it would be harder
to organize and track relationships between multiple people.
A tree would also be less appropriate because trees generally
follow a parent-child structure, while social networks allow
people to have multiple connections without a strict hierarchy.

One performance advantage of using a dictionary is that finding
a person by name takes approximately O(1) average time. Adding
a friendship requires adding each person to the other's friends
list. This takes O(1) time on average when appending to a list,
although checking for an existing friendship takes O(d), where
d is the person's number of friends.

Printing the network takes O(V + E) time because the program
must visit every person and display their friendships. The main
trade-off is that adjacency lists use less memory than adjacency
matrices when a network has relatively few connections. However,
checking whether two specific people are friends requires
searching through a person's friends list.

Overall, an adjacency list provides a simple and efficient way
to represent a social network that can grow as more people
and friendships are added.
